  "summary": {
   "status": "complete",
   "text": "Daniel Glad said companies cannot claim mature AI governance or serious antitrust compliance without examining whether AI tools facilitate competitor coordination and understanding how pricing technology works. He said antitrust rules do not change when conduct moves to software, that software cannot launder collusion, and that Sherman Act accountability remains for corporations and individuals. He described the key question as whether there is an agreement among competitors, regardless of whether the hub is a spreadsheet, platform, or large language model, and said detection capability grows as conduct migrates to software because automated bids, data inputs, and queries leave records. He said the Whistleblower Rewards Program is well-positioned for AI and algorithmic risks and identified software platforms that aggregate competitor data and return pricing recommendations as the most developed area of algorithmic antitrust enforcement.",
   "sids": [
    "s1",
    "s2",
    "s5",
    "s3",
    "s4",
    "s6",
    "s7"
   ],
   "sentences": [
